# What is it that is scarce?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does a choice really cost when you could have done something else? What can Harbourtown's frontier of fish and bread tell us about trade-offs?"
next: {"title":"Is economics a topic or a method?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/03-is-economics-a-topic-or-a-method.cast"}

## The free ticket
sources:
    - id: ferraro_taylor
      title: Do Economists Recognize an Opportunity Cost When They See One? A Dismal Performance from the Dismal Science
      authors: Paul J. Ferraro and Laura O. Taylor
      year: 2005
      finding: Asked the Clapton/Dylan question at the 2005 ASSA meetings, 199 economists answered $0 (25.1%), $10 (21.6%, the correct answer), $40 (25.6%) and $50 (27.6%).

    box tonight "Tonight" width 280 height 100 icon ["crescent moon","moon"] font_size 30 in fork hidden true
    box clapton "Clapton: free" width 280 height 100 icon ["guitar"] font_size 30 in options hidden true
    box dylan "Dylan: $40 ticket" width 280 height 100 icon ["microphone"] font_size 30 in options hidden true
    column options gap 70 in fork hidden true
    row fork gap 220 fit.h 270 fit.w 920 fit.x 40 fit.y 390 hidden true
    arrow to_clapton tonight -> clapton hidden true
    arrow to_dylan tonight -> dylan hidden true
    text worth "worth $50 to you" below dylan gap 20 #2f6b8f font_size 28 hidden true
    text scarce "the scarce thing" below tonight gap 24 #b5482e font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch result cites ["ferraro_taylor"] work ["Economists who chose $10:","21.6 %"] x 330 y 255 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch calc work ["Dylan: $50 of pleasure","− $40 of cash","= $10 given up","= opportunity cost"] x 330 y 255 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The free ticket"

What is it that is scarce? Start with a concert ticket that costs nothing, and ask whether it really costs nothing.
    draw tonight

You have won a ticket to see Eric Clapton. It is free, and you cannot sell it.
    draw to_clapton clapton

B: But Bob (@draw to_dylan dylan@) Dylan plays the same night. A Dylan ticket costs forty dollars,

B: and seeing him would be worth fifty dollars to me.
    draw worth

    pause 0.3

    ask "So what does an evening with Clapton really cost? Pick one of the four answers." poll.choices [{"text":"$0","share":0.251},{"text":"$10","share":0.216},{"text":"$40","share":0.256},{"text":"$50","share":0.276}] poll.source ferraro_taylor store p stage own
        right: You chose {p}, and so did {p.share} of a room full of economists.

    erase poll_1_btn_1 poll_1_btn_2 poll_1_btn_3 poll_1_btn_4

In 2005, two economists put this exact question to about two hundred economists at their own annual meeting.
    draw result_box result_line_1

The answers came out almost evenly spread. The right one, ten dollars, was the least popular: about one in five.
    draw result_line_2

    pause 0.5

B: Ten? The ticket was free. Where does ten come from?
    erase result

From the evening you give up.  (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@)Dylan would give you fifty dollars of pleasure

for forty dollars of cash.
    draw calc_line_2

Skip  (@fade dylan to_dylan worth to 0.3@)Dylan and you lose the fifty, but keep the forty.

What you really give up is ten.
    draw calc_line_3

    pause 0.5

That is opportunity cost: the value of the best thing you give up, net of what it would have cost you.
    draw calc_line_4

    pause 0.3

B: So the ticket was never the scarce thing. (@draw scarce@) The evening was.
    erase calc

Exactly. Prices only keep score; what runs out is the time and effort that could have gone somewhere else.
    highlight tonight effect circle

## Harbourtown's frontier
domain: {"x":[0,470],"y":[0,115]}
vars:
    b:
      value: 100
      min: 0
      max: 300
    cost:
      expr: 100*sqrt(1-(b/400)^2) - 100*sqrt(1-((b+100)/400)^2)

    axes ax x_label "Bread (loaves)" y_label "Fish (crates)" ticks.x [100,200,300] ticks.y [25,50,75,100] hidden true
    curve frontier #b5482e expr 100*sqrt(1-(x/400)^2) x_from 0 x_to 400 hidden true
    label lab_frontier "frontier" right attach_to frontier hidden true
    dot p_fish at.data [0,100] hidden true
    label lab_fish "all fish: 100" above-right attach_to p_fish hidden true
    dot p_bread at.data [400,0] hidden true
    label lab_bread "all bread: 400" above-right attach_to p_bread hidden true
    path s1 #2f6b8f points [[0,100],[100,100],[100,96.8]] data true hidden true
    label lab_s1 "−3" above #2f6b8f attach_to s1 hidden true
    path s2 #2f6b8f points [[100,96.8],[200,96.8],[200,86.6]] data true hidden true
    label lab_s2 "−10" above #2f6b8f attach_to s2 hidden true
    path s3 #2f6b8f points [[200,86.6],[300,86.6],[300,66.1]] data true hidden true
    label lab_s3 "−21" above #2f6b8f attach_to s3 hidden true
    path s4 #2f6b8f points [[300,66.1],[400,66.1],[400,0]] data true hidden true
    label lab_s4 "−66" right #2f6b8f attach_to s4 hidden true
    group stairs members ["s1","lab_s1","s2","lab_s2","s3","lab_s3","s4","lab_s4"] hidden true

    card title "Harbourtown's frontier"

B: Now scale that evening up to a whole town. Harbourtown has, say, a hundred workers, and each day they can fish or bake.
    draw ax

B: If everyone f (@draw p_fish lab_fish@)ishes, the town lands a hundred crates of fish and bakes nothing. (@draw p_bread lab_bread@) If everyone bakes, four hundred loaves and no fish.

B: Every best split in between lies on this curve: the production possibility frontier, the most the town can make of both at once.
    draw frontier lab_frontier

    pause 0.3

So what does a loaf cost here, counted in fish?
    highlight frontier

B: Move enough workers to bake the first hundred loaves, and the town loses only about three crates.
    draw s1 lab_s1

B: The last hundred loaves come from moving the final workers off the boats. Guess first: how many crates do they cost?
    scale guess x 470 y 540 width 420 label "the last 100 loaves cost" unit " crates" min 0 max 100 value 66

    ask "Click on the line: how many crates of fish do the last hundred loaves cost?" on guess store g default "10" stage own voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it is {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is {g.true}. Most people expect the price to stay put.

    erase guess

B: (@draw s2 lab_s2@) Sixty-six cr (@draw s3 lab_s3@)ates. The s (@draw s4 lab_s4@)ame hundred loaves now cost twenty times as much fish.

    pause 0.5

Why so much dearer, for the same bread?
    highlight lab_s1 lab_s4 effect circle

B: Because people differ. The first to sw (@highlight s1@)itch are keen bakers who were poor fishers; the last are t (@highlight s4@)he best skippers in the harbour.

B: That is why the frontier bows outwar (@highlight frontier@)d: each extra loaf costs more fish than the one before.
    fade stairs to 0.3

    hide stairs

Pause here and slide the town along its frontier. Watch the price of the next hundred loaves climb as bread grows.
    dot town #b5482e at.on frontier at.x 100 radius 10 bind {"at.x":"b"} style.fill #b5482e
    label lab_town "next 100 loaves: −{cost:0} crates" below-left attach_to town

    explore params ["b"]

And a point inside the curve, say boats idle during a strike, is the one place where more bread costs no fish at all.
    dot strike #8f887c at.data [150,50] style.fill #8f887c
    label lab_strike "strike: idle boats" below attach_to strike

    pause 0.3

B: Everywhere on the frontier itself, more of one thing means less of another. That is scarcity, drawn.
    highlight frontier

## Will we ever be rich enough?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: You count what you give up, not just what you pay.
    good: Opportunity cost is hiding in plain sight, and you found most of it.
    poor: 'Even economists miss it: four in five did.'
    none: Free tickets are never quite free.
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 1870
      - 1900
      - 1913
      - 1929
      - 1938
      - 1950
      - 1960
      - 1970
      - 1980
      - 1990
      - 2000
      - 2010
      - 2019
      - 2023
    series:
      - {name: UK, values: [2755, 2656, 2656, 2257, 2200, 2023, 1948, 1733, 1654, 1617, 1568, 1522, 1545, 1523]}
      - {name: 'Keynes: 15 h/week', values: [780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780, 780]}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 3000
    x_label: Year
    y_label: Hours worked a year
    points: true
sources:
    - id: owid_hours
      title: Working hours per worker (Huberman and Minns; Penn World Table 11.0), via Our World in Data
      authors: Michael Huberman and Chris Minns; Feenstra, Inklaar and Timmer
      year: 2025
      finding: 'United Kingdom: about 2,257 hours per worker in 1929 and 1,523 in 2023.'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/annual-working-hours-per-worker
    - id: keynes1930
      title: Economic Possibilities for our Grandchildren
      authors: John Maynard Keynes
      year: 1930
      finding: Forecast that the standard of life in progressive countries a hundred years on would be four to eight times as high, and imagined three-hour shifts or a fifteen-hour week.
    - id: maddison2023
      title: Maddison Project Database 2023
      authors: Jutta Bolt and Jan Luiten van Zanden
      year: 2024
      finding: 'UK GDP per person (2011 dollars): 8,673 in 1930 and 38,407 in 2022, about 4.4 times as high.'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-per-capita-maddison-project-database

    dot p1929 at.data [1929,2257] cites ["owid_hours"] hidden true
    label p1929_label "2,257 h" above-right attach_to p1929 hidden true
    dot p2023 at.data [2023,1523] cites ["owid_hours"] hidden true
    label p2023_label "1,523 h" above attach_to p2023 hidden true
    portrait keynes of "John Maynard Keynes" cameo true hidden true
    scratch income cites ["maddison2023"] work ["UK income per person","1930 → 2022: × 4.4"] x 640 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch sure work ["Choices cost: consensus","Why we still work: contested"] x 640 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"C = w\\,(H - \\ell)"},"one more hour off: C falls by w","w = the price of an hour off"] x 640 y 540 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards days bins ["On the frontier","Inside it"] check each hidden true
        * "Every boat at sea, every oven hot" bin "On the frontier"
        Inside it: Boats idle in a strike, Bakers wait for a broken oven
        * "Half fish, half bake, all busy" bin "On the frontier"

    card title "Will we ever be rich enough?"

B: Here is my objection. Get rich enough, and surely scarcity goes away.
    draw axes

Keynes thoug (@draw keynes@)ht something like that. In 1930 he guessed that in a hundred years we would be four to eight times richer,

    erase keynes

and might work about fifteen hours a week.
    draw line_2

    pause 0.3

This line is how many hours the average British worker put in each year. In 1929, about twenty-two hundred and sixty: some forty-three a week.
    draw p1929 p1929_label

    ask "Draw how you think British working hours went from 1929 to today." on line_1 from 1929 store g relative true
        right: Close: on average you were {g.pct} off.
        wrong: On average you were {g.pct} off. Hours did fall, but nowhere near fifteen a week.

B: Hours fell by about a third, to around fifteen hundred a year. That is still about twice his fifteen-hour week.
    draw p2023 p2023_label

Yet the income half of his guess came true: income per person in Britain is about four and a half times what it was in 1930.
    draw income_box income_line_1 income_line_2

    pause 0.5

B: So we got richer and kept working. Why?
    erase income

One answer is opportunity cost again. As wages rose, every hour off got dearer, because it costs what you could have earned in it.
    highlight line_1

B: Another is Keynes's own: some wants are relative. However much we have, we want what the neighbours have.
    highlight line_2

How sure are we? That  (@draw sure_box sure_line_1@)every choice costs its best alternative: consensus.

Why rich people still work this much: contested.
    draw sure_line_2

    pause 0.5

    erase sure

If you want the hour-off arithmetic, open the math. If not, skip it.
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math_box"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "The arithmetic of an hour off: open the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math_box

    erase mathq

Say you have H hours and earn w an hour: the goods you can buy are w times the hours you work.
    draw math_box math_line_1

Take one more  (@draw math_line_2@)hour off, and you give up w. The wage is the pric (@draw math_line_3@)e of leisure, and a raise makes leisure dearer, even as it makes you richer.

    pause 0.5

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    quiz "A student could earn $20,000 a year in a job. Instead she studies, paying $8,000 a year in fees. What does a year of study cost her?"
        intro: Three new cases to finish.
        right: $28,000: the $8,000 she pays plus the $20,000 she gives up by not working.
        wrong: Count what she pays, and also what she gives up.
        * $8,000
        * $12,000
        * $20,000
        + $28,000

    fade axes line_1 line_2 p1929 p1929_label p2023 p2023_label to 0.12

    draw days

    ask "Is Harbourtown on its frontier or inside it? Sort each day." on days store f stage own voice b
        right: All four. Inside the frontier, something sits idle; on it, nothing does.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Inside means something sits idle.

    erase days

    fade axes line_1 line_2 p1929 p1929_label p2023 p2023_label to 1

    quiz "A surgeon earning $300 an hour and a student earning $15 an hour each queue two hours for free festival tickets. For whom is the free ticket dearer?"
        right: The surgeon: two hours cost her about $600 of work, the student about $30.
        wrong: What else could each of them have done with those two hours?
        + The surgeon
        * The student
        * The same: both are free

B: So what is scarce? Not money, and not things.
    highlight line_1 line_2

Time, and everything else it could have gone to. A richer town has a bigger frontier, but it still has an edge.
    highlight line_1

